# Instructional account for the targeted repair

The learner expands a linear combination of two three-component vectors, equates components, solves two independent scalar equations by the source's substitution/elimination route, checks the unused component, and selects the coefficient sum or “No solution.” Lesson step fd740ffd-227c-5f9c-9418-4c54bf1616ff says “Equating the corresponding components” and “Finally, we substitute” before deciding whether the check is true. Its canonical example 13ebde04-8518-5837-9420-c9487debc562 uses c/d names with a consistent result, so names cannot determine consistency. Prerequisite topic 17592186048826 supplies two-equation substitution, including rational coefficients.

Observed source evidence: all 14 supplied practice exercises have one five-choice radio field and moderate labels. q-34568 (14248ec5-b54c-565b-8b43-275a9e097f62) solves the first two rows, then contradicts the third. q-105163 (f9fda870-d2c0-5b67-a720-dd733f4059b5) is an a/b-labelled inconsistent case with candidate coefficients 3 and -11/2. q-105165 (5a605eaf-55a3-544d-8186-239316368511), q-105164 (1ee2b674-88a1-5c58-b73e-36713fef024d), and q-73957 (4ccf091c-7121-5c91-b86a-266cd19f2350) supply the other omitted profiles. Every original complete bank must replay, including two five-numeric banks. All supplied questions, the canonical example and prerequisite context were read. No diagrams or image assets occur in the supplied capture; matrices are TeX.

Fixed features have instructional reasons: two unknowns and a sum preserve the requested operation; three components require a consistency check after a determined two-equation solve; independent coefficient columns prevent nonunique coefficients; source coefficient columns and elimination plans preserve the same arithmetic route. Moderate is supported; easy/hard are unsupported because no source evidence supports those bands. Original labels remain unchanged.

Answer-bearing roles are target membership in the coefficient-column span, the unused row that decides consistency, and the sum's numeric choice rank. Names and bank shape must not encode these roles. Choose consistency and numeric rank before incidental ordering. Keep each source's independent row pair; compute its candidate coefficients, then construct the remaining target component as its predicted value plus a zero or nonzero residual. This conservative extension permits both branches for every coefficient profile without changing the method. In q-34242 a visibly false zero row is decisive mathematical evidence and may legitimately avoid solving; setting that residual to zero requires solving the other rows. This is the source's valid simplification, not an incidental cue.

Retain the bounded translation t=-2..2, adding (t,2t) to the source candidate coefficients; this keeps comparable arithmetic while varying sums. Residuals -1,0,1 are conservative same-method extensions, and exact source residuals are supported for replay/counterexamples. Numeric banks for new instances contain the candidate sum at any of four ranks, three other distinct values at independently sampled positive gaps, plus No solution in both branches. These are an explicitly authored bank extension; original distractor misconception intent is unresolved. Source-only five-numeric banks are preserved exclusively in exact replay, so their absence of No solution cannot predict a freshly generated branch. No claim of source-authored error recipes is made.

Source replay uses {translation:0} and retains every original answer value and worked method. Source q-105163 has a faulty last equality: -3+3(-11/2) is -39/2, not -33/2; its conclusion remains No solution. Correct that displayed check while retaining the source equation, solve and complete bank, and test the correction independently. q-105166's wording omits naming the second equation being added; generated variants name it explicitly. No source evidence has changed since review; missing profiles come from replacing the stale ten-question runtime capture with the supplied fourteen-question capture.

